  Nodal:
    'Nodal establishes the anterior posterior and left right axes, making it one of the earliest lethal morphogens when deleted globally. Adult or organ specific questions therefore require sophisticated conditional, mosaic, or hypomorphic strategies rather than conventional knockout. Timed deletion after gastrulation is essential if you care about later organogenesis rather than axis failure. Knockin alleles that reduce Nodal dosage model human NODAL laterality disorders more gently than nulls. Humanized Nodal is specialized but useful for biologics engaging human NODAL epitopes.',
  Nog:
    'Nog encodes Noggin, a BMP antagonist that shapes joint, neural tube, and skeletal patterning. Complete loss produces severe skeletal fusion phenotypes, so conditional or hypomorphic alleles are usually required for adult joint and hearing studies. Cartilage restricted deletion after joint cavitation separates morphogenesis from adult BMP tone control. Transgenic Nog overexpression remains a classic tool to suppress BMP locally. Humanized Nog supports biologic and gene therapy programs delivering human NOG sequence.',
  Notch1:
    'Notch1 is the mouse form of human NOTCH1 and takes part in Notch signaling in lymphocyte and epithelial fate. A floxed line plus a well chosen Cre is often enough to get usable signaling cohorts. Related pathway members can mask a single locus knockout, so dual conditional crosses are worth planning if redundancy is likely. For antibody work, a human exon knockin at Notch1 can avoid false negatives from sequence drift. Many groups flox Notch1 first, then keep a null only if they need a severity benchmark or a rescue cross.',
